          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em modular lattice
            is a lattice where “opposite sides” of a “diamond” formed by four points
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee y
            are “congruent”.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A modular lattice is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            which satisfies a modular law, which we introduce after a few preliminaries.
          %p
            In any lattice
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in L
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x, y]
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi z
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{z \colon x \leq z \leq y\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjoint pair
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⇆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (-) \colon [a \wedge b, b] \leftrightarrows [a, a \vee b] \colon (-) \wedge b
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (-)
            is left adjoint to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) \wedge b
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \in [a \wedge b, b]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} unit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \leq (a \vee w) \wedge b,
          %p
            whereas for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in [a, a \vee b]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} counit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi z
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (z \wedge b) \leq z.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is
              %strong modular
              if for any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in L
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (-) \dashv (-) \wedge b \colon [a, a \vee b] \to [a \wedge b, b]
            %p
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
          %p This is perhaps the most memorable definition for a category theorist: it is a precise expression of the slogan given in the Idea section.
          %p
            It is immediate that the concept of modular lattice is
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} self-dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is modular, then so is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^{op}
          %h2#alternative_formulations Alternative formulations
          %p In the lattice-theoretic literature, modularity is usually formulated somewhat differently. Here are three alternative conditions on a lattice, all equivalent to that of Definition 1.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong modular law
                is the universal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horn+theory"} Horn sentence
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b \vdash (a \vee z) \wedge b = a \vee (z \wedge b).
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong modular identity
                is the universal equation
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b) = a \vee (z \wedge (a \vee b))
            %li
              %p
                “
                %strong> Freyd’s modular law
                ” (for lack of better term; see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegory"} allegory
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge b \leq a \vee (z \wedge (a \vee b)).
          %h3#proofs_of_equivalence Proofs of equivalence
          %h4#derivation_of_modular_identity Derivation of modular identity
          %p
            To see that the modular identity follows from Definition 1, observe that for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in L
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b) \leq a \vee b
          %p
            Let
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w = (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) \wedge b \colon [a, a \vee b] \to [a \wedge b, b]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            is sent to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b) \wedge b = (a \vee z) \wedge b.
          %p
            Under Definition 1, this last element is sent back to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (-)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi w
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b) = w = a \vee ((a \vee z) \wedge b)
          %p
            and since this is true for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b, z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and rearrange by commutativity to get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge (a \wedge b) = a \vee (z \wedge (a \vee b))
          %p which is the modular identity.
          %h4#modular_law__modular_identity
            Modular law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇔
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Leftrightarrow
            modular identity
          %p
            To get the modular law from the modular identity, just use the fact that the hypothesis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b
            is equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b
            in the modular identity. Conversely, from the tautology
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq a \vee b
          %h4#freyds_modular_law__modular_identity
            Freyd’s modular law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇔
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Leftrightarrow
            modular identity
          %p
            From the tautology
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge b \leq (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            in Freyd’s modular law, we derive the special case
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b) \leq a \vee (z \wedge (a \vee b))
          %p whereas the opposite inequality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (z \wedge (a \vee b)) \leq (a \vee z) \wedge (a \vee b)
          %p holds in any lattice, so the modular identity follows from Freyd’s modular law.
          %h4#modular_identity__definition_1
            Modular identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            definition 1
          %p
            Finally, we derive the adjoint equivalence of Definition 1 from the modular identity. One half of the adjoint equivalence states that if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi z
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq z \leq a \vee b
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = a \vee (z \wedge b)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi z
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq z \leq a \vee b
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = (a \vee b) \wedge z = (a \vee b) \wedge (a \vee z)
          %p
            just by the laws of a lattice. By the modular identity (again switching
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (b \wedge (a \vee z))
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee z = z
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee (b \wedge z) = a \vee (z \wedge b)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b
                in a distributive lattice, we have
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \vee z) \wedge b = (a \wedge b) \vee (z \wedge b) = a \vee (z \wedge b)
              %p which proves the modular law.
            %li
              %p
                For any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mal%27cev+variety"} Mal'cev variety
                or Mal’cev
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
                of an algebra is a modular lattice. The equivalence classes often arise as cosets of kernels; for example, for a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroups"} normal subgroups
                of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideals"} ideals
                of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %li
              %p
                In fact, any lattice of commuting equivalence relations on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                is a modular lattice (being a suballegory of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegory"} allegory
                of sets, one in which composition provides the join).
            %li
              %p
                Every abstract
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+plane"} projective plane
                gives rise to a modular lattice
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                whose underlying set is the disjoint union
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi points
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi lines
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \cup \{1\} \cup \{points\} \cup \{lines\}
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                is taken as bottom,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                as top, the points are atoms, and the lines are coatoms, ordered by the incidence relation. The projective plane need not be Desarguesian.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Young%E2%80%93Fibonacci+lattice"} Young–Fibonacci lattice
          %h2#characterization Characterization
          %p
            The smallest non-modular lattice has 5 elements and is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em pentagon
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\bot, a, b, c, \top\}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq c
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            is incomparable with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Dedekind)
              A lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is modular if and only if there is no injective function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 5
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon N_5 \to L
              that preserves meets and joins.
          %p
            (Notice we are leaving out the condition of preservation of the top and bottom elements.) The direction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            is easy enough:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            being modular is incompatible with an injective
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 5
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: N_5 \to L
            preserving meets and joins, since we contradict the modular law in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            by applying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b \vee a) \wedge c = \top \wedge c = c
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \vee (a \wedge c) = b \vee \bot = b
          %p
            This is reminiscent of forbidden minor characterizations of certain classes of graphs; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+minor"} graph minor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattices"} distributive lattices
            – see this
            %a{:href => "#Lein"} comment
            by Tom Leinster at the
            = succeed "-Category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#free_modular_lattices Free modular lattices
          %p
            Free modular lattices tend to be complicated.
            %a{:href => "#Ded"} Dedekind
            showed that the free modular lattice on 3 elements has 28 elements; its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hasse+diagram"} Hasse diagram
            can be seen in these
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.hawaii.edu/~jb/math618/os9uh.pdf"} lecture notes
            by J.B. Nation (chapter 9, page 100).
          %p
            N.B.: this notion of lattice is meant with respect to the signature
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\wedge, \vee)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triality"} triality
            of this lattice is given in this
            = succeed "-Category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2015/09/the_free_modular_lattice_on_3.html#c049649"} post
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_4
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coxeter+diagram"} Coxeter diagram
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 8
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(8)
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements is infinite and in fact has an undecidable word problem (Freese, Herrmann).
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/allegory"} allegory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthomodular+lattice"} orthomodular lattice
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord
                  geometric lattice
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/geometric+lattice"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semimodular+lattice"} semimodular lattice
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Ded
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"}> Richard Dedekind
              , “Über die von drei Moduln erzeugte Dualgruppe gemeinsamen Teiler”, Math. Annalen 53 (1900), 371–403, reprinted in
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gesammelte mathematische Werke
          %ul#Lein
            %li
              %p
                C. Herrmann, “On the word problem for the modular lattice with four free generators”, Mathematische Annalen 265 (1983), 513-527. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.springerlink.com/content/w862594668m2n375/"}> Springerlink
                )
            %li
              %p
                J.B. Nation,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Revised Notes on Lattice Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.hawaii.edu/~jb/"} web
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
                , Comment on Solèr’s Theorem, December 4, 2010. (
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/12/solers_theorem.html#c035900"}> link
                )
